WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:00.849
Harvest now,

00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:05.344
decrypt later is the risk that an attacker collects encrypted traffic or

00:00:05.344 --> 00:00:09.839
stored encrypted data today and saves it for possible decryption in the

00:00:09.839 --> 00:00:10.489
future.

00:00:10.489 --> 00:00:14.609
The attacker may not be able to read the data now.

00:00:14.609 --> 00:00:18.729
The concern is that future capability, including cryptographically relevant quantum capability,

00:00:18.729 --> 00:00:22.475
could expose data whose confidentiality still matters years from now.

00:00:22.475 --> 00:00:25.096
This risk depends heavily on data lifetime.

00:00:25.096 --> 00:00:29.217
If information only needs to remain confidential for minutes or days,

00:00:29.217 --> 00:00:32.213
future decryption years later may have limited value.

00:00:32.213 --> 00:00:37.082
If information must remain confidential for decades, the risk is much more important.

00:00:37.082 --> 00:00:40.828
Government data, healthcare records, legal records, intellectual property, trade secrets,

00:00:40.828 --> 00:00:41.952
critical infrastructure details,

00:00:41.952 --> 00:00:45.697
and sensitive personal information may all have long value windows.

00:00:45.697 --> 00:00:48.694
Encrypted network traffic is one area of concern,

00:00:48.694 --> 00:00:52.065
especially when sessions depend on quantum-vulnerable public-key key exchange.

00:00:52.065 --> 00:00:53.938
Stored encrypted data is another.

00:00:53.938 --> 00:00:56.185
Backups, archives, database dumps, document repositories,

00:00:56.185 --> 00:01:01.054
and captured files may remain sensitive long after the original system is retired.

00:01:01.054 --> 00:01:05.549
Security teams should understand which data has long confidentiality requirements and where

00:01:05.549 --> 00:01:07.796
encryption depends on vulnerable key establishment.

00:01:07.796 --> 00:01:11.167
The response is not panic collection of new tools.

00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:12.291
It is prioritization.

00:01:12.291 --> 00:01:17.535
Start with data that has long sensitivity, high adversary interest, and exposure to collection.

00:01:17.535 --> 00:01:21.281
Then identify the systems, protocols, certificates, keys, and vendors involved.

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:25.775
Harvest-now-decrypt-later risk becomes manageable when it is tied to concrete data classes

00:01:25.775 --> 00:01:29.620
and migration plans rather than treated as a vague future worry.
